Phillip Helbig wrote:
> 
> In article <F34LML.Frz@spcuna.spc.edu>, Terry Kennedy
> <terry@spcunb.spc.edu> writes:
> 
> > Phillip Helbig <helbig@man.ac.uk> writes:
> 
> > > Is there any way to test for devices, like with SH DEV at the
> > > >>> prompt on newer VAXstations, on this beast?  It would be nice to
> 
> >   I'd suggest carefully opening the box and seeing what's in there first.
> 
> I have opened the box and know what's inside; it's just that SH DEV
> shows you what the machine can see etc and thus lets one know if perhaps
> something is there physically but not functioning etc.

The KA630 CPU's firmware doesn't have anything like SH DEV.  If
you suspect it's a MSCP-emulating controller, you can enable
the Q-bus I/O page mapping and see if something responds at
a standard MSCP CSR, but that doesn't help a lot unless you've
already looked in the box and figured out that there's nothing
else that can be responding at a MSCP CSR.  You can also
boot VMS and do a MCR SYSGEN SHOW /UNIBUS, which will effectively
scan I/O space for you (assuming it doesn't crash if it
accidentally triggers an unexpected interrupt!)

Still, as Terry said, you should open up the box and see what's
connected there.  Anything else won't be nearly as useful!

Tim. (shoppa@trailingedge.com)



