Simon Coombs wrote:
> > >Does anyone have any idea what that small, 8-pin connector is over the
> > >video-out connector on a VT220? It hides under that little panel, and I
> > >had been wondering what it's supposed to do for *years*...
> 
> I'm beginning to wonder if it's there to control what comes out of the
> 'video out' connector... I've yet to find something that a VT220 will
> connect to succesfully without rolling, wrapping the picture round, and
> being generally unuseable.

Remember, most monitors designed for home use are a 4.5MHz NTSC signal,
suitable for use to display a regular television air signal.  However,
studio NTSC (and most A/V) equipment is almost always 6MHz, and will cause
saturation problems on non-studio or non-A/V (audio/video) monitors.  This
goes for most older terminals; the vendors assumed that you would be using
A/V style monitors to display their output.

I have a premium Sony Trinitron XBR television at that can handle this --
it is basically an A/V monitor (including the Panasonic A/V connector used
with the old 1/2"-reel-to-reel VTRs) that can handle 5 different video
inputs, one of which is a built-in RF tuner (fully cable-ready at that for
how old my set is).  It has no problem displaying the video output of an
old VT220.  Since it is 25", and I help out a video-taping production crew
now and then (they do a local cable station, weddings, etc), I carry it a
lot to our productions as a display monitor.  The video effects mixer
("wiper board") has a 6MHz NTSC output, and my Sony handles it too.

So try this: borrow or rent an A/V or studio-grade monitor and connect it
to your VT220 and see if your problem goes away.  If not, open the terminal
up, and using the same borrowed monitor, adjust the convergence trimpots
to fixup the video for best output.  Or, knowing that that would screw up
your built-in display, look for a bad capacitor.  These older type units are
notorious for capacitors going out; though this can be a great deal harder
to find the bad one.

-- 
Scott G. Hall
