In article <732fet$d6i$1@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>,
Eric Werme - replace nospam with werme <> wrote:
>It's something like 3 bit data tracks on either side of the timing track
>down the center.  (The data tracks are copies, so you generally had to
>punch big holes in the tape to force a read error.)

More or less, it's 3 data tracks plus a timing track (just clocks) and a mark
track (which carries six-bit number telling the significance of the adjacent
18 data bits -- the mark codes are chosen carefully so that they work as
sync characters too, and blocks are symmetric so that the obverse complement
mark codes are the same in reverse).  All are duplicated for 10 tracks total,
but I think the hardware isn't smart enough to do anything special with the
dual diversity, the heads are simply wired in series.  Could be wrong about
that though, anyway yeah it's still enough to make up for dropouts.

>If the -6 used the same format as the -10, then the
>RT-11 FILEX program can read the files.

N.B. FILEX supports text files only on PDP-10 tapes.

John Wilson
D Bit



