The unthinkable has happened. My DECwriter-III has stopped working. 

Those things aren't supposed to *break*! They're *DECwriters*! You're 
supposed to be able to shelter under one in the event of a direct nuclear
strike, while still printing!

I'm in need of a replacement logic board. If anyone has a spare, 
functioning board that they'd be happy to part with, would you 
drop me a line, with a suggestion on price? I'm in the UK... TIA.

In case anyone is interested, basically it would appear that the 
carriage-control half of the logic has gone 'phut'. A bit of amateurish 
prodding with a meter showed that the board offered 0 ohms of resistance 
to the PSU, and that it was 2 power transistors (Probably??) that had 
gone. I still don't know why - I have no idea how I got as far as I did, 
let alone how to find the cause!

Anyway, I managed to locate another knackered LA120 logic board that I'd 
had squirrelled away for years, and did a transplant. The thing now 
lives, in a powers-up-if-you're-lucky-but-don't-try-to-print-more-than-
-five-lines kinda way.

It really is a testament to the simplicity of design of the DECwriter-III
that an unskilled oik such as myself should be able to get *this* far 
with it - it may just be an old printing terminal with a yellowed case 
and a noisy fan, but to me, the joy of ownership has never worn off...

Simon Coombs.

