> >let alone how to find the cause!

> If it's the two transistors I'm thinking of, I believe they drive the head
> carriage (which is a PM motor *not* a stepper like the LA30 etc.) pretty
> much directly so if they've gone poof, better find out why before plugging
> in another new board.

Those are the two; an MJ2500 and a 11349. 

I've tested the PSU; the voltage matches that of my other, running 
DECwriter. Mind you, this is the second PSU it's had this week; the LA120 
came out of storage a week ago, and after giving it time to warm up a 
little to avoid condensation, it was turned on. It came to life, 
although the printhead only made it part of the way across the platen 
before stopping with a flashing '8' on the keyboard. 

After turning off and on, it managed it completely, albeit slowly, and a 
quick test print later saw the head moving across the page steadily 
faster and faster, until it was operating at a normal speed. I then left it
idling for an hour to warm up properly and drive out any moisture, and came 
back to find... nothing. The PSU had packed up. I've still to look into that.

Fortunately, a spare came to the rescue. I still don't know if the board 
killed the PSU, or the PSU weakened the board, or what! After the PSU swap
was done, it was run continuously for 3/4 of an hour with no problems. 
Then a week later - oops.

>                       What's the symptom exactly?  I had problems with
