Simon Coombs <simon@nenevr.demon.co.uk.NOJUNK> wrote:
>Also, with the printhead at the LHS of the carriage at start 
>up, it normally jumps about 1/2" to the left, before returning to its 
>normal position; a couple of times, it has moved only a tiny amount to 
>the left, before returning... odd.

I *think* the purpose of that is that the micro is searching for a
micro-switch at the left margin, so that it can zero its counters and use
the encoder wheel to keep track of head position from then on.  The fact
that it gets satisfied earlier than usual might mean something, you might
want to see if the micro-switch (I *think* that's what it uses, not an
optical thingy) is doing the right thing.

>A little more prodding with a meter at the bad and a good DECwriter have 
>shown that the output of the 2 transistors on the good DW while active 
>is 16V, and on the other board, 24V. That's quite a difference!

I stink at diagnosing stuff like this so it would probably be better to listen
to *anyone* else, but it seems to me that what you need to know now is whether
the bad board is giving 24V on purpose (which IIRC is the supply voltage,
or is it +/- 24V in a push-pull setup?).  I.e., is it giving 24V because one
(or both) transistor has shorted out, or is it giving 24V because the encoder
wheel still hasn't seen the head move to the proper position so it's yelling
at the head motor trying to get it moving.  If the motor is really seeing 24V
and isn't moving then that seems like a fried motor (does it have brushes?
those might be bent or dirty) might be the explanation.  I'm not sure I'd
really recommend it but I tested my motor once by jumpering it straight to
the power supply for a split second -- WHACK!

John Wilson
D Bit



