In comp.sys.dec John Wilson <wilson@dbit.com> wrote:
> I *think* the purpose of that is that the micro is searching for a
> micro-switch at the left margin, so that it can zero its counters and use
> the encoder wheel to keep track of head position from then on.  The fact
> that it gets satisfied earlier than usual might mean something, you might
> want to see if the micro-switch (I *think* that's what it uses, not an
> optical thingy) is doing the right thing.

  Well, neither (sort of). It uses the absence of encoder pulses to tell it
that it's reached the left margin. When it sees the pulses stop for a bit,
it backs the head off about 1/2" and declares that the left margin.

  Anecdote: I actually saw a shop where the users set the left marging by
powering up and grabbing the head!

  Anyway, old DECwriter II/III's have dried-out oil on the carriage and
get stuck (or move slowly), which can cause odd margin positions as well as
"mooing" of the alarm.

  The microprocessor reset circuit on this puppy isn't that bright, and
brief power blips will leave the CPU side of the house dead, with the
carriage drive in full-bore mode. This causes the carriage fuse (top right
of the logic board, as I recall) to blow, leaving you with a terminal that
moos continuously on power-up, blinking the "Paper Out" LED, and a carriage
that is not locked (no stepper power).

  You can pop the toothed belt off the stepper for testing purposes (use the
spring thingy on the right side to reduce the belt tension first). That way
if you get a stuck-on transistor you won't blow it by locking up the motor.
It's also a good way to check for the motor shaft binding.

