  The threat to tell a long winded story about core reminds me of a
story that the DEC technicians here in Houston were relating, circa
1975 or so. They were asserting that there was site in town where a
disgruntled programmer had written a toggle in program that would
"ruin" core memory in his company's 11. They claimed that, once loaded,
it would take control of all trap and power up vectors, and refuse
to let anything else run. They claimed that they couldn't clear it
no matter what they did, and had to send the boards back to Woburn,
where the core memory test jig could rewrite 'em.

  It made for an interesting story at the time, and they seemed earnest,
but, looking back with 21 years of experience, seeing how it's just not
possible, I wonder if they themselves believed this story, or were all
having me on...the DEC techs in town at time were a wild bunch, and were
certainly capable of telling a few whoppers about computers at the expense
of a gullible young operator (me, that is)...

  Anyone else hear this tall tale? Was it a widespread technical urban 
legend, or just the product of a local fevered imagination?

