Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com> writes:
>[...his usual crud...]

OK Andrew, time to lose the noise and get real.

I'm a customer, ready to buy and I invite you to bid for my money.

1. I expect to spend about US$3K (although I might part with up to US$5K).

2. I want a workstation (which you can call a server or potato or
   whatever you like) for use at home, with SCSI disk and the usual
   odds and ends.

3. I need a proper development environment installed.  I define this
   to mean any decent Unix with the standard collection of tools
   including a C compiler and an assembler (GCC and GAS would be fine).

4. I intend to use it for coding arithmetic routines that operate on
   big integers, polynomials over finite fields etc.  I want it to run
   this code quickly.

5. I want email support from clueful people in case of trouble (no I
   don't want to phone Lola's Astrology and Tech Support Hotline, thanks).


It more or less has to have 64-bit integer registers but VLM is
irrelevant (for me "big" is up to 100 MB or so); floating-point is
irrelevant.

I don't know of any benchmarks that model my needs very well.  For
instance SPECint95 only uses small integers and has too small a memory
footprint, whereas SPECfp95 relies a lot on f.-p.  Something like
(SPECint95+SPECfp95)/2 is a rough measure.  Speed of GMP is better,
but my own code is the final word.

I'm a fan of Digital hardware (due to experience, not bigotry) but not
their prices.  I've found Alphas running D.U. and Linux to be reliable
and fast but I'll consider the best deal from just about any vendor.


Currently I'm considering a 633 MHz Alpha machine with Linux which is
$2795 from www.dcginc.com, and adding a bit of extra RAM.

Can you go faster for less?

Don't bother going off on yet another dismissive tangent.

Just put up or shut up.

Rob.



