In <Pine.BSF.3.91.981126180446.22942A-100000@fanta.iet.unipi.it> Marco Guidi <guidi@fanta.iet.unipi.it> writes:

>If I try to compile and link it with
>
>    cc a.c
>
>I get an a.out executable file that I can run.
>
>But if I try to  compile a.c with 
>  cc -c a.c
>
>and then I try to link with 
>  ld a.o -lc
>
>I get an a.out executable file, but when I run it
>I get a Segmentation fault error.
>
>Is there anyone who can help me understanding why this not works ?

The easy way is to not explicitly invoke ld yourself.  Use cc to perform
the linking, like this:

  cc a.o

It you need/want to use ld, use the following command to discover how to
correctly invoke ld for a C program:

  cc -v a.c

The generated ld command might look like this:

/usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ld -g0 -O1 -call_shared /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/crt0.o a.o -lc 

The magical ld incantation is fairly system specific, so people tend to
avoid it and use cc instead.

Dan
