In article <365213EF.9BC46522@bigsky.net>, Rohn Wood <rohn@bigsky.net> writes:
|>I inherited responsibility for an Alpha AXP 150 with 128 meg. in the
|>following config:

[snip - basically 2 banks, 4 slots/bank, 16MB (4MBx36bits) in each slot]

|>
|>When we remove any two banks of memory--from the system -- the system
|>fails to post?  the only way it boots is with the memory slots fully
|>populated.
|>

According to the manual, the maximum memory is 128MB achieved using
eight 4MBx36 SIMMs and this seems to be what you have. To achieve 64MB you
need four 4MBx36 SIMMs in Bank 0.

The rules are:

	- 36-bit SIMMs rated @ 70ns
	- Bank 0 must be occupied.

Now I haven't been inside the Jensen in the lab for a few days so I do not
know whether the banks are labelled or not, but the diagram in the manual
shows that the slots are interleaved to achieve each bank. In my poor ASCII
art the best I can do is:

	<Connector for BBU?>    ------------ bank 0
                                ------------ bank 1
                                ------------ bank 0
                                ------------ bank 1
                                ------------ bank 0
                                ------------ bank 1
                                ------------ bank 0
                                ------------ bank 1

Even if you cannot identify the banks from my drawing, there are clearly
only two ways of putting four SIMMs into every other slot and one of them
must work.

I hope!

If you are still stuck, send mail - I need to open up our Jensen in the
next few days so I can take a peek while I'm in there.

Antonio

