Manufacturers make disk drives for the market place and also make disk
drives for various OEM's.

Disk drives made for Digital/Compaq are branded with Digital/Compaq part
numbers. These have been extensively evaluated and tested and revised for
OEM use.

You are correct, there are firmware differences in these drives, such
things as broken commands or fixes to improper SCSI phase executions or
little bugs that result in the SCSI bus having to be reset to re-establish
communicaiton with a drive or even the level of error reporting and
possibly the process of Bad Block Replacement or even the number of blocks
reserved for Bad Block Replacement.

That all said, many SCSI disks will work with OpenVMS, but if they are not
branded by Digital/Compaq, they have not been tested and so support
becomes an issue. In addition, the consumer variant of a disk drive may
not perform optimally with OpenVMS in comparison to the same unit with
Digital/Compaq firmware.

