In article <siwfwZ4shKAh@mcrcr6>, smithp01@mcrcr6.med.nyu.edu writes:
>We have started dabbling in multi-processor software and obtained a database 
>search program that is meant to run using all available CPUs in the box. We 
>have a 4-CPU 4100, VMS 7.1, Patches include ALPPTHR02, ALPSYSA02, ALPBASE02 
>but we don't have ALPTHREADS_03.
>
>With help from many people (some of whom may be reading this here) we managed 
>to get the programs running in multi-thread mode.  This was excellent news.  
>But the software on an empty machine only seems to use 2 of the four CPUs.

This doesn't happen to be FASTA does it?  If it is, you might have a look
at thr.h, where you will find:

#ifndef MAX_WORKERS
#define MAX_WORKERS 2
#endif

which could be the source of your problems!  You will also notice
in that case in MAKEFILE.UNX you will find on the CC line "-DMAX_WORKERS=4".
On the make_vms.com that I have (a couple of years old) there is
a /define for MAX_WORKERS=4, but I built that one by hand, and the default
VMS build procedure (if there was one, don't recall), may not set that.

