X-Deja-AN: 412464703
References: <72nrd3$5jm$1@probity.mcc.ac.uk> <364FA474.E5858835@videotron.ca>
Organization: The World Public Access UNIX, Brookline, MA
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> writes:

>Phillip Helbig wrote:
>> 
>> Someone has given me these two items.  Can I connect this disk to this
>> machine?  Do I just need a cable to connect it to the strange connector
>> on the VAXstation which looks different than the rest?  Why does the
>> disk have 3 connectors on the back?

Sounds like you have an RD54 in a 'leprechaun' box (a table-top unit
with power supply).  If so, the three connectors on the back of the
desktop box are

	MSCP DRIVE 0 IN

	MSCP DRIVE 1 IN

	MSCP OUT

If you do indeed have a leprechaun box, the cable which comes from the
disk controller (an RQDX3) would plug into the top connector for the
first RD drive.  If you had a second unit, you would need a second
cable which plugs into the bottom connector of the first unit, and into
the middle connector of the second unit.  The connector is three rows
of pins, a total of about 50 (but I'm not sure).

As for the connector on the back of your uVax, *which* connector are
you talking about which doesn't look like the others...

>As far as vaxstations are concerned, I beleive you need a Q bus expansion box,
>and in that box, you might need the RQDX3 card (disk controller) as well as the
>disk drive itself.

If you have a BA23 box, then you can have one or more RQDX3 controllers
plugged into the backplane.  If you need more backplane space, *then*
you use another box, and a backplane jumper.

>Does the Vaxstation II have a built-in RQDX3 controller for RD drives ?

Normally it did, but depending on what the prior owner did to it, it
may no longer have it.  We'd need to see a listing of the board
numbers (the M-numbers from the handles of the boards) which are plugged
into the backplane (and note the position, it is important).

Bottom line is that even if you have one RQDX3 in the unit, you could
plug in another one and, with the proper cables, attach the external
RD54 and use it.

					Megan Gentry
