Info on the MMJ connector found on various DEC terminals & computers.

--------------- --------------- --------------- --------------- ---------------
DEC uses a connector for its serial connection called an MMJ, which
I assume stands for 'Modified Modular Jack'.  It is like a six conductor
modular telephone connector, only the key on the connector is offset,
not in the center like a standard telco connector.

Looking at the back of a VT320 for example the connector jack looks
something like this.

   --------------------
   | *  *  *  *  *  * |
   --------------    --
                |____|

     1  2  3  4  5  6    are the pinout numbers

--------------- --------------- --------------- --------------- ---------------
The signals are as follows (with respect to a VT320, VT420, etc):

#  I/0  Desc
1   >   DTR  Data Terminal Ready
2   >   TXD  Transmit Data
3   -   TXD- (ie Gnd)
4   -   RXD- (ie Gnd)
5   <   RXD  Receive Data
6   <   DSR  Data Set Ready

> = signal output from terminal
< = signal input to terminal

--------------- --------------- --------------- --------------- ---------------
DEC makes several adapters to adapt the MMJ to 9 and 25 pin D connectors.

The H8575-A is an MMJ (female) to DB25 (female) and breaks out thus:

MMJ	DB25
 1 	 20
 2	  2
 3	  7
 4	  7
 5	  3
 6	  6

I have used the above using a standard DEC cable with MMJ's on both ends
and the adapter to connect to a PC 25 pin serial port, using the PC
as a terminal.


The H8571-F is and MMJ (female) to DB25 (male) and breaks out thus:

MMJ	DB25
 1  	  6
 2	  3
 3	  7
 4	  7
 5	  2
 6	 20


There is also a MMJ to 9 pin D connector, part number H8571-B.
I have never seen one, so have not actually checked how it is wired, but
given the other info here, should not be difficult to discern.

--------------- --------------- --------------- --------------- ---------------
I had DEC MMJ cable that someone cut one end off so I wired it to a 9 pin D
connector myself to use with a PC 9 pin serial port.  Wired as follows.

MMJ	DB9
 1	 NC
 2	 2
 3	 5
 4	 5
 5	 3
 6 	 4

This was to use a PC as the console on a DEC 5400, DEC 5500, etc.
Realize that this is wired for the MMJ port on the computer, not the terminal.
Also MMJ pin 1 not connected as DSR line not needed in this situation.

--------------- --------------- --------------- --------------- ---------------
This is for female 9 & 25 pin D connectors for using a PC as a terminal
connected to computer (ie the console port of a DEC 5400 for example).

Term  Term  MMJ 				       MMJ port on DEC
DB25   DB9			        	       computer or Decserver

 20     4    1 --->-------------DTR---------------->--- 6
  2     3    2 --->-------------TXD---------------->--- 5
  7     5    3 ---------------------------------------- 4
  7     5    4 ---------------------------------------- 3
  3     2    5 ---<-------------RXD----------------<--- 2
  6     6    6 ---<-------------DSR----------------<--- 1



Note how the wires of the MMJ 'turn over' from one end to the other.

