The only way to partition a disk to install for Windows NT on an ALpha is,

1. Use the AlphaBIOS if your system has AlphaBIOS.
2. Use ARCINST.EXE if your system does not have AlphaBIOS.

I personally have attempted to use some offline Intel type utilities such
as Partition Magic and Ghost and neither of these work with any great
degree of success.

The principal reason for the above programs not working is as follows.

Microsoft Disk Partitioning is not a completely documented entitity. Many
various firms have figured out how to do it, empirically along with some
sketchy documentation that Microsoft has thrown to the masses.
One important thing is that Disk Parititons and Partition Maps must begin
and end on cylinder boundaries. SCO UNIX and NOVELL Netware take advantage
of this fact.
As SCO UNIX and Novell Netware were never ported to Alpha, the Alpha Disk
Partitoning is not encumbered by this afformentioned fact. Thus, Windows
NT ALpha DIsk Partitioning does not start and end on cylinder boundaries,
thus Ghost and Partition Magic complain that the partitions are broken,
when in fact they are not.

