You specifically say ARC, therefore I will respond as such, realize of
course, most machines of recent vintage have an AlphaBIOS that is capable
of doing what you want.

Iregardless, with the ARC and AlphaBIOS, you have the capability to run a
program from the console.

You can select run a program and type,

cd:\alpha\arcinst.exe

and this will run that program from the CD-ROM.

The program has a limitation of dealing with a 2GB maximum size. In
addition, it does not produce the reccomended partitions easily.

Let's do an example.

Say you have a 2GB disk drive. Make it into two partitions, the first
partition should be 2GB - 10MB in size. Then, make a second partition, 10
MB in size. Delete the first partition and then go back and make the
second partition a System Parition. This is extremely important, as this
stamps that 10MB parititon as a System Parition and also sets a pointer in
the ARC console, which in fact is a hidden variable that you cannot see.

The start the install process for Windows NT and when you get to the
section about setting up your disk drives, create your first partititon
here. If you get a complaint that there is no systems partition, it means
that you did not successfully use ARCINST to make that 10MB system
partition. go back and try again.

rtt

