Paul Thompson <thompson@athenet.net> writes:
> I believe TU81's have a SDI interface and require a HSC disk controller or
> other SDI controller to operate.

  Urrm, that's the TA81 and it speaks STI to "tape channel" controller cards.

  The basic Tx81 hardware is a 1600/6250 CDC (MPI) drive with a Pertec inter-
face (formatted, as I recall). The TU81 added a LESI interface and the TA81
added a STI interface. The -Plus versions of these drives added 256KB of cache
memory which the host drivers did an *abysmal* job of using (it was common for
performance to plummet when the drive was "upgraded). Later drivers were better
at handling the cache.

  The TU80 (no TA80 or -Plus versions) is a 1600-only CDC/MPI drive with a
formatted Pertec interface. The only DEC controller was the M7454 Unibus
controller (an OEM'd Dilog DU13x), but it worked on just about any 3rd-party
Pertec controller. The DQ132 was popular on Q-bus systems.

