In alt.sys.pdp11 Tim Shoppa <shoppa@trailing-edge.com> wrote:
> As long as the experts seem to be paying attention:
>
> Raising the questions: what *is* a TU82?  What is "special
> high density"?
>
> Later on, references in the magtape density tables are
> made to the "RV80", "RV20/RV60", and "TM32".  These were
> all clearly (T)MSCP devices, but I've never been able to figure
> out what they actually are!

  I guess I should answer this (even though I'll have to kill you after I tell
you 8-).

  Vernon put all of those (and some you didn't spot) in for me. In particular,
I wanted to use an RRD40 and its controller on a RSTS/E system, but got the
dreaded "unrecognized controller/device type, device disabled by INIT" at
startup. So I asked him to add all of the know [T]MSCP device types, on the
theory that disks/tapes shouldn't be disenfanchised just becayse RSTS/E INIT
didn't know about them.

  Vernon was on the MSCP Architecture Committee, so many of the devices list-
ed were devices that did not exist as production units, but might have been
planned at one time (so a device code was entered).

  The RV20 existed - it's a large platter optical disk (WORM) drive. The RV60
was to be a double-sided drive (the RV20 used double-sided media, but you had
to take the cartridge out and flip it over).

  "Special high density" is the Cipher 3200BPI mode (for example, on the F880).
There were a number of projects to make a lower-cost and smaller TMSCP 9-track
product (compared to the TU81+, which looked kind of silly standing next to a
PDP-11/93 in a BA23). Eventually the TMSCP requirement got dropped (when DEC
no longer saw supporting this new drive on PDP-11's as necessary) and the drive
that actually came out was the SCSI TSZ05.

  I'm not sure about the TM32 - but it may be the formatter in a TA81. Remember
that Vernon added the whole device list, not just those that could be attached
to a PDP-11. The TM78 (TU78/TU79) formatter is in there as well (in the Mass-
bus section).

