	If memory serves, its one or more flip flops, possibly a high
	speed register.

A Bit of History
	(no guarantees on accuracy....)

	Cast the mind back to 1955.  The IC is still a lab curiosity.
	'digital technology', born of the computer switch and relay
	interlocking on the RRs has run up thru vacuum tubes, and is
	inching tis way to solid state.  A designer CAN sit down and design
	gates and registers and then, from them design a 'puter.  Or whatever.
	
	Some would rather skip step one, and Just Do Digital Design.
	A number of companies (Digital, for one) did step one and sold
	the end product.  (and other products...   8)>>)  Think of the
	flipchip as sort like a funny shaped IC, with all the insides showing.  

	The flipchip was second or third generation.  First was a 'system unit'
	roughly 4x the card area, metal frame.  Next were 'flip chips' [1]
	of which the B210 seems to have been a double height specimen.  the
	Nominal standard was 3 by 5 (approx inches....).  There were, from
	memory:

		A Series	Analog, amber handles.  analog muxes, amps,
				a-d & d-a Converters.

		B Series	'fast' digital, discretes, rtl (?). Blue
				Handles

		R Series	'normal' digital, discretes, rtl(?), red handles

		G series	memory sense amps, slicers & wierd stuff.
				for core, when core was core  8)>>  Green
				handles.

		K series	'slow' (noise immunized), logic, intended
				for indutrial control/relay emulation,
				black handles.

		M Series	TTL-chips on cards.  magenta handles.
				(The advantage over using raw chips was the
				range of standardized connectors, etc...)

	All, were used in internal design, and by late M so much was on a card
	that it was a complete option.  Long about M timeframe connector went
	to doublesided (had been 18, now 36).

	Within each series (especially B, K, R and early M) there was structure
	to the numbers.

		*1xx was gates
		*2xx was flops/registers
		*3xx was delays
		*4xx was clocks
	(at which point i ferget...   8)>>)

	This is a brief summary, w/o Actually looking it up, as brief as can
	& still be (i think 8)>>) coherent...

