Early 1988 release date, KA825 processor board (same as the VAX 8250/8350),
RD54 drive (with a VAXBI controller, the name & number of which escapes me
right now), Evans & Sutherland graphics, about 90kg or thereabouts. Single
phase, basically an under-the-desk BA123-type box except with a VAXBI instead
of a Q-bus. Probably rarer even that the VAXstation 3520/3540 (and slower too,
of course).

I only ever saw one, and that was at university. I don't know how long
they lasted. VMS dropped support somewhere after V5.3, so they were obviously
short-lived.

The codename was Lynx (I know I'm not supposed to acknowledge such things
but as a customer I had VAX 8200 memory boards turn up in boxes marked
"Lynx memory" so I feel on fairly safe ground here).

Antonio

