In article <72gbt2$37q$1@news.usit.net>, "Kent Rankin" <kentrankin@theatreorgans.com> writes:

>     I have the ability to buy 5 of these at surplus cost(ie. incredibly
> inexpensive, kind of below surplus cost).  Could anybody tell me about them?
> 

>     Is this one of the more modern DECServers? Or one of the ones from the
> eighties?

    From the 80's (1985 or thereabouts)

> How many ports does it handle, and at what speed?  Is it one of the LAT-only
> models?

    8 ports ( via a "harmonica" attached to a Centronics style connector 
on the unit), data leads only ( Tx, Rx and Gnd I believe - no modem control ).
Maximum speed 19.2K. LAT only.

> Does it require VMS on the host in order to run?

   VMS or Digital Unix ( or perhaps a 3rd party DECNet/LAT implementation for some
other Unix system if those are still being sold ). Needs DECNet on the host
(MOP protocol) to load the DECServer software.

> What is one worth?

   Not much ( maybe $25-50??)

>  And even better(as I certainly couldn't use five of them
> myself), is there any demand for them in the market?
> 

   Very limited - many people are throwing more modern DECServers away.

