I've got an AlphaServer 4000 here running NT4.0, that persists in
configuring more than one PCI card with the same interrupt, even though
there should be plenty available. It's got the latest AlphaBIOS and SRM, and
nothing I can do (except moving cards around between slots) will keep it
from doing this. Although the system seems to be working OK, I'm a little
leery of having both the primary RAID array's host adapter and the tape
drive's host adapter on the _same_ interrupt.

Anyway, here's the configuration:

PCI BUS 0:
  Slot 0: #9 764 video card supplied by Digital
  Slot 1: Adaptec AHA-2940UW (brand new, latest BIOS, with SCSIBIOS
emulation turned on)
  Slot 2: 3Com 3C509-TP ISA (on I/O 300 and IRQ 10)
  Slot 3: Empty

PCI BUS 1:
  Slot 0: Empty
  Slot 1: QLogic ISP1020 supplied by Digital
  Slot 2: Adaptec/Cogent EM-964TX (four 10/100 Digital Ethernet ports on the
other side of a Digital PCI-PCI bridge)
  Slot 3: Empty

There is also the internal PCI bus containing the built-in Symbios Logic
SCSI host adapter.

At the moment, the IRQ configuration is the following (for non-ISA
interrupts):

AIC78XX: 0x25
QL1020: 0x25
EMPCI1: 0x29
EMPCI2: 0x2A
EMPCI3: 0x2B
EMPCI4: 0x2C
SYMC810: 0x31

As you can see, there are plenty of interrupts available, but the system
persists in configuring Slot 1 of both busses at 0x25... If I move the
Qlogic card to Slot 0 of Bus 1, then it gets a different, but still shared,
interrupt. I've not yet tried moving it to Slot 3 of Bus 1 (or moving the
Quartet to that slot), but that's next on the agenda.

The question is, why the heck is it doing this???




