In article <rjqr9vc52hw.fsf@mermaid.ucc.gu.uwa.edu.au>, Paul Repacholi <prep@ucc.gu.uwa.edu.au> writes:
> 
> I have been given a pair of DEcstations,
> but have no KB or mouse. Can anyone
> fill me in on what these things really are?

Sounds like you have a Personal DECstation.

The lk-501 keyboard is mechanically very like an LK-401, except that
instead of an rs-232 interface, it uses a `desktop bus' with a
different connector. Like Apple's ADB, only completely different.  The
keyboard plugs into the CPU box, the mouse daisychains off the keyboard,
and they send event-packets to the CPU over the desktop bus.

Oh, and the mouse I have says its a VSXXX-GB, not -BB.


The Personal DECstation PROM only undertands the LK-510 and mouse; not
even the standard (DEC) LK-2xx/4xx RS-232 keyboard or mice. And
there's only one serial port, anyway, so you cant add both a serial
keyboard and a serial mouse, even if you kludged up OS support.  So,
unless you can find an LK-501 and mouse, forget about using the
displays on these machines.

