Presumably this is because:

A. Compaq is still afraid, as was Digital, that people would use 
"workstations" in place of "servers".  (This can't be a workstation, 
there's no epoxy in the backplane slots?!)  The Compaq VMS folks REALLY
need to get that concept out of their heads.

B. Compaq isn't paying attention to Intel dual slot workstation sales.
(Hard to believe, but maybe the right hand doesn't know what the left is up 
to.)  The last 5 Intel workstations we bought were dual slot, single
processor.  In 18-24 months we'll buy second processors for all of them. 
By that time the price of 400 Mhz processors will likely be less than half
of what it is now, and so we'll roughly double the performance for a very
moderate investment.  It should keep the machines in service for maybe a
year longer than they would have otherwise, and it's a much cheaper way to
go than buying the two CPUs at the beginning of the machines' lives, when
they are most expensive.  Getting a 2X CPU boost on that single slot
VMSstation is going to cost a lot more, since you're looking at buying a 2X
faster single processor, and likely also a whole new motherboard to support
it.  This is the sort of consideration which will tip potential sales away
from VMSstations. 

(this one I find very hard to believe, but....)

C.  The dual pc264dp dual processor board won't work unless both slots are 
filled.  The PII dual processor boards won't work with an empty slot
either, but the active adapter that plugs in to keep it happy is very 
inexpensive when compared to the PII it replaces. 

