This is probably a pointless idea, but does the behavior change depending
on whether you've enabled unit attentions?  At least that gives the controller
something to do with a disk changes, maybe it'll get you through a different
code path in the firmware and get better behavior.  Although, CMD controllers
don't poll the SCSI device so the attention is delayed until the next attempt
to do I/O on the device, dunno if Dilog is the same.

If this is on a PDP-11, DUTEST might be a handy way to poke around the
controller instead of having to get carried away instrumenting your driver.
http://www.dbit.com/pub/pdp11/rt11/dutest.mac.  It can be loaded by RT-11,
but it's a stand-alone program so you can use something else instead (like
the RSTS INIT.SYS "LOAD" command) as long as it comes up in kernel mode,
and it has a simple command language that allows building and queueing
command packets and reading response packets.

