Here's a summary of what I've done with two RZ55 drive and one RZ56
drive:

Dilog SQ739 on a MicroVAX II running VMS or NetBSD or a KA650 system
running NetBSD:  bits get flipped randomly on writes.  Reads are okay.
(Is the controller broken?)

Unknown Dilog SCSI controller (no identifying marks or diag messages): 
one RZ55 works.  Other RZ55 and the RZ56 both get inexplicable write
errors when doing newfs or trying to install VMS on them.  Actual writes
to the block device in NetBSD don't return errors, but bytes get changed
in semi-predictable ways.

DECstation 3100 running Ultrix 4.4: everything works.

VAXstation 3100m38:  ROM routines work with it, but NetBSD has timeouts
and "no REQ" error messages when trying to detect drive.  Eventually
nothing gets detected.

PC with Adaptec AHA1510 SCSI controller:  Linux locks up when detecting
the drive.  In fact, it seems that this locks up the AHA1510, and it
requires power cycling.  With MS-DOS and the latest Adaptec drivers
everything works perfectly.  The latest drivers even spin up the disk if
necessary.

Now, I think I can assume that the drives aren't broken because they
work in some situations.  My question is:  What the hell is going on? 
Are the drives actually SCSI?  Are Dilog SCSI controllers any good?  

Oh, and one more question:  Is there some conspiracy here to force
people to use all-DEC systems? :-)

BTW.  Yeah, I did reconfigure the drives for spin up on power up when
dealing with SCSI implementations that are too brain-damaged to actually
send a start unit command.  That generally didn't help.

