Okay, I finally got the drives to work with Linux.  Apparently the
problem was that you *must* do a synchronous data transfer request
(SDTR) negotiation with the drive, even if you want to do asynchronous
transfers.

I was using asynchronous because the Linux aha152x driver can't properly
do synchronous.  It never did the SDTR negotiation, so data transfers
never worked.  After I hacked the driver so it negotiated asynchronous
using the SDTR negotiation then everything worked.  Linux even spins up
a drive if necessary.

I don't want to use these with Linux... I need them for VAXen.  I just
wanted to do some more testing.  rzdisk and scu can't do random seek
tests, so I used sformat running under Linux.  Anyways, the drives pass
*all* tests.

Now, could this SDTR negotiation stuff be the reason why NetBSD/vax has
those problems when detecting the drive on a VAXstation 3100m38?  Could
it be the reason why weird things happen when I try to use them with
Dilog SCSI controllers?  How could I get the drives to just default to
asynchronous?  (I've checked jumper settings and mode pages but I can't
see anything)

(Yeah, I'm is assuming it's not just cabling on the Q-bus systems,
because swapping cables or terminating at a different drive makes no
difference whatsoever.)

