In article <724458$i8f$1@news.usit.net>, "Kent Rankin" <kentrankin@theatreorgans.com> writes:
> 

>     Anyhow, now he needs information on the 8350.  Can anyone help here?

What kind of info?  The 8200 series use the VAXBI as backplane and
(IIRC) memory bus. The CPU boards are, in essence, basically the
microvax-II chipset, but with extra chips to handle the entire Vax
instruction set (string instructions, packed decimal, etc) that're
missing in the microvax subset) plus a BI interface. The 8300 is a
two-CPU 8200.  The 8250 is a clockrate-enhanced 8200, and the 8350
is a dual-cpu 8250.

I hope your friend got either two KDB-50s, or a vaxbi CI and an HSC,
to handle all those SDI disks.

A microvax 3100 with scsi disk would be smaller, and quieter, and (for
just about anything except a COBOL timesharing workload) faster -- but
not cheaper.



