>    Anyhow, now he needs information on the 8350.  Can anyone help here?



---- 8000 series (1986)
----+---+-----+-------+--------+------------------------------------+-------
------------------
SID | X | Id     | Speed |  Bus   | Model Name
| Nickname
-----+---+------+---------+--------+--------------------------------+-------
------------------
 05 |   - | 8SS | 0.9-2   |  B,C    | VAX 8200, 8300, 8250, 8350     |
Scorpio

This is a Dual CPU system.

I didn't see a console in there.  One of these should have a Dec Pro350 or
similiar (PDP?)
as a system console, (sort of control panel) that is used to load the o/s,
control the boot etc.
You need that, plus the console software. And the cable linking it to the
VAX.  (Vital!)

An SA482 sounds like a cabinet for the drives rather than a 1.8gb drive.
You list 3 RA82 620mb drives, 3 x 600 =1800 = 1.8Gb. :^), so they are
probably in the
SA482. The solitary RA81 might be the boot disk.
The RAxx drives have a plastic plug with a drive number on it,
in the top right corner of the front panel, which doubles as the
drive id setting plug, so don't move them around!
The boot drive will probably be the lowest number, usually 0.

I didn't see a Heirarchical storage controller in this lot, so I take it
there is
a KDB50 SDI controller in the machine and a KLESI-B or similar for the TU81?
That's a bonus, from your point, as a HSC chews about 700w on it's own.
(They are 3 phase as well)

Make sure you get ALL the cables.  Black square section ones are for the SDI
drives to the
disk controller.  If this system has a console, there will be a multiplex
double cable from it to
the VAX backplane.  There will also be a biggish D connector cable from the
TU81 to the controller.

>    Also, is this one of the VAXes that required tri-phase power?

Yes, I believe so.

> If so, could someone point me to that document that says how to convert it
to
>single-phase?

Don't know of one, someone else may. This may not be as easy as you think.
Particularly if you only have 110v US house current to work with.
 I have converted a few 6000 class vaxen to single phase 240vac (Aust house
current)
but to make a 220v 3 ~ system run on 110v single ~  will be (IMHO) more
difficult.  Depending on the
power requirements of the system, (don't believe the labels on the mains
connector though, it ain't that high!)
you may have a few problems.  These produce some heat as well, tough I don't
think they use ECL.
You might want to consider an air conditioner for the room it lives in.
 (Again, not having a HSC helps a lot, as they produce a fair bit).
Bear in mind that you don't need to run ALL of this stuff to make it work.
A minimal system might include the VAX, the console, and a terminal or 2,  1
or 2 disk drives and a printer.
That should be all you need to play around as much as you like.
The tape drive you can connect and leave switched off unless you need to use
it
for backups or to install software etc.
Grab all the 1/2" R-R tapes you can, they are getting hard/expensive to get.
VMS/VAX is pretty much "plug & play" (windoze eat your heart out)
as far as adding stuff while the system is running is concerned.

There are very likely many here much more expert than I in this particular
class of machine.
So ask all the questions you like.  Before doing so though,  you should
really check the
VMS FAQ at  ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms
Amongst other things, there is information in the FAQ on how to change the
SYSTEM account
password so you can log in, which may well be your first question once you
get it set up and running...

There is also an extensive VMS/Digital resource at
http://multivac.jb.man.ac.uk:8000/helbig/BOOKMARKS/VMS.HTML
with many links to useful information.

Tell your friend not to be too easily discouraged, as biggish iron like this
is getting a bit rare, and
should really be preserved.  Besides, it's more fun than restoring an old
car, and usually, the bits are
available a lot cheaper.  And they do look impressive. :^)

If I can help in any way, sing out.


