Jerome Fine replies:

I have also have some experience with Zip drives using MSCP host
adapters.  In addition, I also am using a customers system which
has a 3 1/2 " floppy (RX23 emulation) connected to a CMD host
adapter.  When the floppy is not in the drive, the system does
hang waiting for the floppy to be inserted;  after the floppy is
inserted, the system does continue properly.

I don't have the system with the Zip drive running right now, so
I can't really remember what happens if the CMD host adapter
is able to recognize what to do if the Zip cartridge is not in the
drive.  But from memory, I seem to remember having the
same hang-up as the Dialog host adapter.  On the other hand,
another customer who originally installed the CMD host adapters
in his systems has switched to Emulex UC07 host adapters which
seem to poll the ZIP drive as to whether or not the cartridge is
present.  When I talked to CMD about this problem, they answered
that the technology on the PDP-11 was old and that there were
no plans to change the firmware.  Note that on the VAX, a VMS
removable media must be MOUNTED which often locks (if
possible) the media in the drive.  This was the case with the
Sony optical platters.  But again, under RT-11 or TSX-PLUS,
there does not seem to be the proper co-ordination and hand
shaking between the firmware on the host adapter and the
device driver.  If the Sony optical platter was not present,
the system would hang.

Incidentally, which version of RT-11 or TSX-PLUS are
you using?  And do you want to boot from a non-zero
partition of the Zip cartridge (after the initial hardware
boot of course).

I assume from Tim's response that you are running RT-11/
TSX-PLUS on your PDP-11.  If you are not (e.g. RSX-11),
possibly the same situation applies.

