Organization: neuron, technology consultants
NNTP-Posting-Date: 5 Nov 1998 00:18:57 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

I just had the opportunity to closelt examine two different Compaq
Proliant Servers and a Compaq Storage enclosure.

It must be the marketing group that designs this stuff. The concept behing
some of the high availability ideas is questionable at best. For example,
special fans in one unit, back to back accessible from the top of the box.
I had a fan fail on me, oh, about 8 years ago. These things are mounted in
proprietary brackets too, and they look like some special fan as well.

A giant mother of a power switch on the front of the box, covered by an
equally large and obnoxious plastic shroud. Duh, when was the last time
you let an idiot inot your computer room, to open a locked cabinet and
press the power switch. Heck a simple recessed switch would have been more
elegant and nicer looking too.

The thing that really slammed me, was the disk drives. There they were, in
all their glory, 9.1GB UW, 10,000 RPM and 4.3GB UW, 10,000 RPM. Anyone
ever have look at the Compaq drives in their nice little case. Take a
close look at that paddle board the SCA connector plugs into. Let's do
some math, SCSI has 8 control signals, and in narrow mode, has 8 data
lines. In UW, or Ultra Wide, SCSI has 8 control signals and 16 data lines.
Look closely at that paddle board again folks. There are not enough
control / data signals to run that UW drive in wide mode. Oh sure, the
system has a UW controller, the connectors for the cabling outside all the
boxes is UW capable, but those drives plug into a narrow backplane and
therefore, they run in narrow mode.

rtt

