My understanding is that the phone support is free for 3 years from the dateof
purchase. If you have a problem, ask to speak to the manager on duty.

> >The Starion 9xx series was designed to be industry compatible in the area of
> >motherboard form factor (the chassis is designed on a standard called
> >"Western Digital LPx"
> OK, excuse my ignorance, but what is LPx?
>

I'm not exactly sure what the initials LPx stand for, but it designates a
particularstandard developed by Western Digital back about 1994. The standard
refers to
a specific size of motherboard and where each mounting screw is located, the
placement of the I/O connectors on the back, the location of the riser and a few
other items. It is similar is scope to the AT spec (again, form factor, mounting,
etc.).
It is not AT compatible.


> I'd really like to know how to disable the onboard video though. We
> installed a new 4M video card and the old 1M onboard video refuses to
> go away. There are no jumpers, that I can find, to disable this chip.
> Am I supposed to disable it in the Advanced Chipset Control of the
> BIOS setup? Because that's the only other thing I could find.
>

The on-board video will sense if there is another video card plugged in and will
direct the video ouptput to the new board. If the video is coming from your
new card, then the on-board has disabled itself. Even though the BIOS disables
the on-board video, the S3 chip that we used is still detected by Windows. You
can complete the disabling but checking the "disable in all hardware profiles"
check box under the Device Manger/Display Properties screen. You do not need
to remove it (if you remove from Device Manager it will just reappear the next
time
you boot).

regards,

