Jerome Fine replies:

Then how about this?

If there is sufficient response, say around 5 to 10 purchases seems
to be reasonable, then I suppose that this could be a starting point:

(a)  Minimum set of Y2K patches for V5.4G of RT-11 including:
      (i)   RT11XM.SYS  or  RT11FB.SYS  (user supplies their answer
             file if a non-standard answer file has been used)
      (ii)   One tape device handler
      (iii)   DIR.SAV
      (iv)   PIP.SAV
      Price is $ US 399
      Delivery is after a license agreement has been signed and the user
      satisfies me that they have a valid license to use V5.4G of RT-11

(b)  Additional set of Y2K patches for V5.4G of RT-11 including:
      (i)     LINK.SAV
      (ii)    LIBR.SAV
      (iii)   IND.SAV
      (iv)   BUP.SAV
      (v)    MACRO.SAV
      (vi)   Other tape device handlers
      Price is $ US 399

(c)  If a reasonable number of users who wish other utilities
      such as QUEUE.REL  and  SP(X).SYS, these will also
      be considered and most likely done.  An evaluation
      will have to take place.  Delivery date would depend
      on the number of copies purchased.
      Probable price is $ US 199

A complete set of Y2K patches will consist of (a), (b) and (c).

(d) Volume arrangements for sites or companies who have
     a large number of users will be available when the first
     purchase is being made.

(e) The Y2K patches have been tested under both real
     PDP-11 hardware as well as the E11 emulator by
     John Wilson.

(f) There will be future support.  Any bugs found in the
     Y2K related bug fixed portions of the programs will
     be fixed.

(g) Other bugs will be considered, especially if the fix
     is relatively straight forward.

This thought is totally pie in the sky.  Since it seems
unlikely at present that even 10 purchases will occur,
I can't see a very large number taking place.  However,
if more than a total of 100 purchases occur, volume
discounts will be applied.  As an example, if there
is an order for 1000 sets of Y2K patches of any
version from V5.3 to V5.6 of RT-11 with a minimum of
250 sets for any given version, a price of $ US 140
per set of Y2K patches for most desired versions will
be considered reasonable so long as the distributed
version of RT11FB or RT11XM was used.  User
provided custom answer files would be allowed at
an extra change.  It is highly likely that at such a volume,
the source code for the patches would be placed
in the public domain within a few years. Thereafter,
the Y2K patches would be free.  Ian, do you want to
handle the marketing for 1000 sets?

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
Year 2000 Solutions for RT-11 Operating System and Applications
(Sources not always required)




